WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.350 align:start position:0%
 
the death toll from germany's  devastating flood disaster has risen to  more than 160  as emergency workers continue to search  for dozens of people still  unaccounted for some experts say it was  a failure of germany's flood warning  system that led to such widespread  devastation they say authorities knew  what was coming  but failed to prepare  among the many questions these floods  have left in their wake  is one about the warnings did people  understand that lives were in danger  from heavy rainfall  those who were there say information was  chaotic  a fire service vehicle came through but  you couldn't understand what they were  saying  i asked a neighbour he said we should  turn off the electricity  because water was coming but nobody  realized it would be a flood more than  two meters high  there were advanced warnings about heavy  rain and so on but some of the warnings  were wrong  they told us there was another flood  coming so we went up the hill  but there was nothing as invalid  but the head of the national disaster  response agency visiting the region on  monday  said the system had worked well  our warning system worked in each and  every case the german weather service  the flood centers and the local  authorities  made intensive use of it 150 warning  signals were sent by  our system  some of those warnings were sent out  using phone apps  but a nationwide test of the system in  2020  showed that does not always work some  warnings came through  half an hour late germany also has a  more old-fashioned network of  sirens from cold war days many of these  have been dismantled in recent years  but around fifteen thousand are still in  operation  yet even such a simple technology is not  foolproof  they sound the siren once every month  but not when something like this happens  i don't understand  it in view of so many deaths it's not  surprising  some people are demanding changes but  for now  the main task is to clean up and to  begin  to learn the lessons  our reporter julia sardelli is on the  ground covering the latest developments  she joins us from the town of antana  in the german state of  rhineland-palatinate which was  especially hard hit  by the flooding  so julia what is the situation there  today  yes it's going to be another day of  cleaning up for the residents here and  for the volunteers who came to help  the lower part of town here behind me  that which is along the river has been  completely destroyed  a road has been swept away a bus over  here has  landed on the shore so the amount of  devastation is really astounding  and one has to imagine that so many  towns like this one have  an equally high level of destruction so  the tragedy is really widespread here in  the region and it's not just a tragedy  of destruction of objects it's  also the loss of human life so here in  the state of rhineland-palatinate 117  people have been  found dead so far many are still missing  and unfortunately the authorities expect  the death toll to to rise still while  while they're  they are clearing up the sellers and the  destroyed homes and they expect to find  more bodies  so cleanup efforts are underway there  but i understand many residents in the  area  are still enduring tough conditions tell  us more about that  yes many people here have lost  everything they have lost their home  they have lost their livelihoods because  restaurants  shops hotels have all been destroyed and  many people are very worried for their  immediate future also in economic terms  but even the people who are lucky and  whose homes are still intact  have a hard time living here there's no  electricity there's no gas  there's no water so it's really hard to  charge one's phone  to wash up after the cleanup or even to  find the functioning toilet  now we heard some angry voices in that  report just a moment ago  people saying they were given  insufficient warning  about this blood are authorities  considering any changes to the warning  system as a result of this disaster  well what we've heard until now is both  federal and local  authorities saying that their prevention  warning system worked that they did send  out warnings to the population and that  they  knew that this was coming they said  there might be some lessons to be  learned but now is not the moment to  be looking at that but here on the  ground speaking to people  it feels like the communication was at  least spotty  some people said the firemen came and  warned them and they ran to the hills  others said the firemen came too late  and there was nowhere for them to go  while others really didn't really grasp  the extent of the flooding that was  about to hit them  julia thank you very much for your  reporting that was julia saudelli there  in  altanar our reporter emily gordeen is on  the ground covering the latest  developments she joins us now from the  town of sure now  in the southern german state of bavaria  so emily what's the situation where you  are today  so right now we're in an area that was  evacuated in light of the floods on  saturday  and now what you see behind me is people  tidying up you can see behind me here on  my right  is um the mud that came down from the  hill together with the flood  literally reached until the windowsills  here and you can see  diggers trying to to free the area of  the mud that came down  you can see that the relief agency the  federal relief agency the thw  working in line with um with the local  fire brigade  and even if you look on my left here now  what you will see as well is that the  military the local the from the local  base here came as well to help to dig  the gardens and and what people had you  know out of the mud  so it's really it's a very tense  situation  um people are still can't quite come to  terms with what has happened because  this is extreme  and um you know and at the same time  it's all hands on deck people are  are you know um distributing little  sandwiches to  to you know to thank the helpers that  are here so  it is intense but people are  trying to really just get on with it now  the flooding  did claim lives in bavaria i understand  but not nearly as many as in neighboring  states  was that just a matter of luck or the  result of better  disaster preparedness  well i spoke with a leader here of the  local fire brigade and he said that  it also comes down to just being a bit  of a scaredy cat as soon as they  hear uh of a big torrential rain then  they um immediately you know they act  um but also he said that it has to do  with the settlement structure here right  so this area where we're in right now  has about 20 to 30 houses and then there  comes nothing for a while and then you  have again settlements of 20 to 30  houses so  the extent is going to be smaller and at  the same time what you need to know here  is that  this is still an extraordinary situation  this is we're standing here  at the at the cusp of a hill of a  mountain really  and um what you what you you know  usually  if there's like a little a little stream  coming down the hill you know that'll  then be bigger and turn into a flood  really but  this mountain here doesn't even have a  really big you know a stream where you  could say oh we can expect more rain to  come down here so  you if you look at to my right here  where the mud is and everything this is  where this huge stream must have come  down and it because of the torrential  rain you know because it was so strong  it took with it um little rocks and  everything debris  with it so it really turned into this  this must like  sort of flood to go that landslide so it  is really quite extraordinary what's  happened here  and just briefly emily um there's been  massive  loss of property there destruction of  property people have  lost their homes or livelihoods what  kind of  help can they expect from authorities  well i mean on the federal level they  have promised to to  put up to 300 million euros um  you know in relief but that is for all  of germany so not just for bavaria  bavaria itself has promised about 50  million  so there is help but at the same time  you know floods aren't necessarily  uncommon in this area  um the big issue that was dealt with a  few years ago or that came up is  you know how much does the state pay and  how much should people prepare for it  um in terms of insurance and some of the  help that that is being put forward from  the state is also  you know dependent on whether people  have insurance but if you're living in a  in a flood prone area then your  insurance is going to be extremely high  and not everyone can afford it and in  some even  some areas even it's the question of  will insurances even put up with the  risk  so that's the question that needs to be  solved in the future  emily thank you very much dw's emily  gordeen there reporting from chernow  bavaria  and joining us now from luxembourg is  jeff decosta he's a researcher focusing  on flood warning systems at the  university of redding  thanks for being with us so german  authorities  insist their flood warnings worked even  though there was  massive loss of life what's your  assessment did the warning system  function the way it was intended  well um thank you for this great  question  uh actually if we look at the aftermath  of last week's disaster um it is quite  evident that the warning systems were  not successful  in the way that um maybe the german  authorities would have hoped that they  would have been  um so i do think um there are some  there are certain uh problems with the  ways that um  this crisis was dealt with and the  warning systems were not  effectively issued okay so there  are problems there you say what lessons  then do you think  should be learned from this disaster  when it comes to flood warnings  i mean there should be a coordinated  effort and  a clear transmission line between the  warnings given by scientists and the  decisions that are taken by decision  makers underground  this is a problematic that is not  specific to germany but it is  a worldwide phenomenon some countries  deal with it better than others  but in this crisis we have clearly seen  that this is not a localized phenomenon  and it's really important that now we  come together and try to find ways  in how we can protect ourselves and  prevent further such outcomes or even  worse  you've been personally affected i  understand by events as your family home  in luxembourg was flooded can you tell  us a bit more about what happened there  absolutely um it came well  my parents called me very late at night  they were very distressed  and i went there as fast as i could and  as safely as i could to help them build  water out  but it was it was a sad scene to see i  mean it was my childhood home  all the memories submerged underwater  and just to see how emotionally draining  it is also for flood victims  is a sad reminder of just how  devastating floods are uh on people  and societies as a whole can you tell us  in a situation like this how the flood  system the flood warning system  is supposed to work i mean assuming that  the  those who were monitoring conditions  actually knew what was coming  was is it how is that supposed to be  communicated to the people on the ground  to get them  out of harm's way  thank you so um there are um  flood warnings in place that are  european but  also local authorities like weather  services for instance in germany as well  that issued warnings  uh that were just as accurate as the  pan-european warning system  and notification system apologies and  the thing is it's not just about the  warning but also about the preparedness  and how resilient the population is  in terms of the vulnerabilities that  they have and the exposure  to risk that they have this is an  assessment that needs to be made  and the decisions need to be taken  according to to these vulnerabilities  based on the warnings that decision  makers get and it shouldn't be up to  them  to decide whether a situation is  dangerous  for their inhabitants or for the  citizens of that particular place  in order to make a wise decision about  to evacuate or not to evacuate people  need to be prepared there needs to be an  educational part  in which people know exactly what to do  in case of a crisis  because as we all know when there is an  emergency situation  people do not react rationally so  especially if something comes as a  surprise um people  need to have this sort of knowledge  beforehand  sort of like a fire drill if you will we  all train  um for a fire drill in case of an  emergency  so that we know do in case panic strikes  we know exactly  what we have to do and this is sadly not  the case  not just here but everywhere across  europe and  beyond i think there's going to be a lot  more discussion about this uh  coming jeff decosta from the university  of redding thank you very much  thank you very much for having me

